## Comparison of Pipeline A (Bowtie2) and Pipeline B (Standalone Python/`edlib`)

Two methodological patterns emerge when comparing Pipeline A and Pipeline B:

1. **Threshold sensitivity on E19:** Under Pipeline B, E19 on-target classification decreases monotonically with stringency: {{e19_pb_m1_on_pct}}% (M1) $\rightarrow$ {{e19_pb_m2_75_on_pct}}% (`edlib` $\ge 75\%$) $\rightarrow$ {{e19_pb_m2_80_on_pct}}% (`edlib` $\ge 80\%$) $\rightarrow$ {{e19_pb_m2_85_on_pct}}% (`edlib` $\ge 85\%$), while ungapped matching yields {{e19_pb_ungap75_on_pct}}%. At $\ge 80\%$, Pipeline B agrees with Pipeline A ({{e19_pa_ontarget_pct}}%) within 0.01 pp at the aggregate level, though their retained read populations differ ({{e19_pb_classified}} vs. {{e19_pa_classified}} classified pairs).
2. **Divergence on PS2 and PS3:** On PS2 and PS3, Pipeline B assigns 3.2–4.4 pp fewer on-target reads than Pipeline A (PS2: {{ps2_pb_m2_75_on_pct}}% vs. {{ps2_pa_ontarget_pct}}%; PS3: {{ps3_pb_m2_75_on_pct}}% vs. {{ps3_pa_ontarget_pct}}%) and slightly higher dimer fractions ({{ps2_pb_dimer_pct}}% vs. {{ps2_pa_dimer_pct}}%; {{ps3_pb_dimer_pct}}% vs. {{ps3_pa_dimer_pct}}%). Pipeline A aligns long reads with Bowtie2 *before* applying the 8-bp $5'$ primer prefix hash, retaining amplicon reads with minor $5'$ errors, whereas Pipeline B filters {{ps2_pb_unmatched_pct_raw}}% and {{ps3_pb_unmatched_pct_raw}}% of raw reads as primer-unmatched. Retaining open-source Bowtie2 therefore tracks the original procedure more closely.

## Cell-by-Cell Matrix Validation (PS1–PS3) and Heavy-Tailed Concentration (E19)

To move beyond aggregate percentages, we compared all six reconstructed 96-plex dimer pair tables (archived under `data/matrices/` with directional columns `reads_p1_p2` and `reads_p2_p1` alongside unordered totals `dimer_reads`, preserving the full ordered $192 \times 192$ matrix and 18,528 unordered pairs) cell-by-cell against both conditions in Supplementary Data 3 (`MOESM7`; Table 3, Figure 2B). Because Pearson correlation can be dominated by high-count pairs and all-cell exact agreement is inflated by structural zeros, Table 3 reports Spearman $\rho$, exact agreement restricted to the active-pair union (**Act. Exact**), active-pair Jaccard overlap, MAE, and normalized L1 total absolute error ($\sum |r_i - p_i| / \sum p_i$).

| Panel | MOESM7 Sheet | Ratio | Pearson $r$ | Spearman $\rho$ | Act. Exact | Jaccard | MAE | Norm. L1 | Top-10 |
| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| PS1 | `conc opt.` | {{ps1_pa_mv_conc_optimized_ratio}} | **{{ps1_pa_mv_conc_optimized_r5}}** | {{ps1_pa_mv_conc_optimized_rho}} | {{ps1_pa_mv_conc_optimized_exact_act_pct}}% | {{ps1_pa_mv_conc_optimized_jaccard}} | {{ps1_pa_mv_conc_optimized_mae_all}} | {{ps1_pa_mv_conc_optimized_norm_l1}}% | {{ps1_pa_mv_conc_optimized_top10}}/10 |
| PS1 | `not opt.` | {{ps1_pa_mv_not_optimized_ratio}} | {{ps1_pa_mv_not_optimized_r}} | {{ps1_pa_mv_not_optimized_rho}} | {{ps1_pa_mv_not_optimized_exact_act_pct}}% | {{ps1_pa_mv_not_optimized_jaccard}} | {{ps1_pa_mv_not_optimized_mae_all}} | {{ps1_pa_mv_not_optimized_norm_l1}}% | {{ps1_pa_mv_not_optimized_top10}}/10 |
| PS2 | `conc opt.` | {{ps2_pa_mv_conc_optimized_ratio}} | **{{ps2_pa_mv_conc_optimized_r5}}** | {{ps2_pa_mv_conc_optimized_rho}} | {{ps2_pa_mv_conc_optimized_exact_act_pct}}% | {{ps2_pa_mv_conc_optimized_jaccard}} | {{ps2_pa_mv_conc_optimized_mae_all}} | {{ps2_pa_mv_conc_optimized_norm_l1}}% | {{ps2_pa_mv_conc_optimized_top10}}/10 |
| PS2 | `not opt.` | {{ps2_pa_mv_not_optimized_ratio}} | {{ps2_pa_mv_not_optimized_r}} | {{ps2_pa_mv_not_optimized_rho}} | {{ps2_pa_mv_not_optimized_exact_act_pct}}% | {{ps2_pa_mv_not_optimized_jaccard}} | {{ps2_pa_mv_not_optimized_mae_all}} | {{ps2_pa_mv_not_optimized_norm_l1}}% | {{ps2_pa_mv_not_optimized_top10}}/10 |
| PS3 | `conc opt.` | {{ps3_pa_mv_conc_optimized_ratio}} | **{{ps3_pa_mv_conc_optimized_r5}}** | {{ps3_pa_mv_conc_optimized_rho}} | {{ps3_pa_mv_conc_optimized_exact_act_pct}}% | {{ps3_pa_mv_conc_optimized_jaccard}} | {{ps3_pa_mv_conc_optimized_mae_all}} | {{ps3_pa_mv_conc_optimized_norm_l1}}% | {{ps3_pa_mv_conc_optimized_top10}}/10 |
| PS3 | `not opt.` | {{ps3_pa_mv_not_optimized_ratio}} | {{ps3_pa_mv_not_optimized_r}} | {{ps3_pa_mv_not_optimized_rho}} | {{ps3_pa_mv_not_optimized_exact_act_pct}}% | {{ps3_pa_mv_not_optimized_jaccard}} | {{ps3_pa_mv_not_optimized_mae_all}} | {{ps3_pa_mv_not_optimized_norm_l1}}% | {{ps3_pa_mv_not_optimized_top10}}/10 |

Against the `conc optimized` matrices, Pipeline A achieves Pearson $r = {{pa_conc_opt_r6_min}}$–${{pa_conc_opt_r6_max}}$, normalized L1 error of {{pa_conc_opt_l1_min}}–{{pa_conc_opt_l1_max}}%, active-pair Jaccard overlap of {{pa_conc_opt_jacc_min}}–{{pa_conc_opt_jacc_max}}, exact integer agreement on {{pa_conc_opt_exact_act_min}}–{{pa_conc_opt_exact_act_max}}% of active-union cells ({{pa_conc_opt_exact_all_min}}–{{pa_conc_opt_exact_all_max}}% across all 18,528 cells including shared zeros), total count ratios of {{pa_conc_opt_ratio_min}}–{{pa_conc_opt_ratio_max}}, and 10/10 overlap among the top-10 pairs (Pipeline B achieves Pearson $r = {{ps1_pb_mv_conc_optimized_r5}}$, ${{ps2_pb_mv_conc_optimized_r5}}$, and ${{ps3_pb_mv_conc_optimized_r5}}$). Direct count stratification against `MOESM7` shows that {{pa_conc_opt_low12_cell_min}}–{{pa_conc_opt_low12_cell_max}}% of disagreeing cells have $\max(\text{rec},\text{pub}) \le 2$ reads (contributing {{pa_conc_opt_low12_l1_min}}–{{pa_conc_opt_low12_l1_max}}% of total L1 reads), whereas high-count cells ($\ge 10$ reads) contribute {{ps1_pa_mv_conc_optimized_ge10_l1_pct}}% (PS1), {{ps2_pa_mv_conc_optimized_ge10_l1_pct}}% (PS2), and {{ps3_pa_mv_conc_optimized_ge10_l1_pct}}% (PS3) of total L1 count differences despite small fractional offsets. By contrast, correlations with the `not optimized` matrices are far lower ($r = {{pa_not_opt_r_min}}$–${{pa_not_opt_r_max}}$ across PS1–PS3), resolving an undocumented provenance detail: the Figshare `E14` FASTQs correspond to the concentration-optimized PS1–PS3 libraries.

In the 384-plex E19 library, dimer formation across the {{e19_pb_total_pairs}} candidate unordered pairs is highly sparse and heavy-tailed (Figure 2A; no formal power-law fit is claimed). Under Pipeline B, {{e19_pb_zero_pairs}} pairs ({{e19_pb_zero_pct}}%) yield zero dimer reads, {{e19_pb_active_pairs}} pairs ({{e19_pb_active_pct}}%) exhibit $\ge 1$ read, and {{e19_pb_severe_pairs}} pairs ({{e19_pb_severe_pct}}%) exhibit $\ge 10$ reads. A single reverse–reverse pair (`{{e19_pb_top1_p1}}` $\times$ `{{e19_pb_top1_p2}}`) accounts for {{e19_pb_top1_reads}} of {{e19_pb_dimer_n}} dimer reads (**{{e19_pb_top1_pct}}%**; top-10 pairs: {{e19_pb_top10_pct}}%). Under Pipeline A, the same dominant pair accounts for {{e19_pa_top1_reads}} of {{e19_pa_dimer_n}} dimer reads (**{{e19_pa_dimer_top1_pct}}%**) across {{e19_pa_dimer_active}} active pairs. Although low-count active pair totals differ between pipelines ({{e19_pa_dimer_active}} vs. {{e19_pb_active_pairs}}), the dominant interaction and strong concentration of dimer reads are robust across both pipelines.

![Pair-level primer-dimer structure and matrix validation. (A) Log-log rank-abundance curve of the {{e19_pb_active_pairs}} active dimer pairs in E19 (generated from archived data/saddle_384_dimer_pairs.tsv), showing heavy-tailed concentration in rank-1 pair {{e19_pb_top1_p1}} x {{e19_pb_top1_p2}} ({{e19_pb_top1_pct}}%). (B) Cell-by-cell Pearson correlation across all 18,528 unordered 96-plex pairs against published MOESM7 sheets, verifying strong matrix agreement (r >= {{pa_conc_opt_r6_min}} for Pipeline A) and identifying the Figshare E14 files as the concentration-optimized libraries.](article/figures/fig2_dimer_concentration_and_matrix.png)

## Matched-Pair Uniform Random Audits of Alignment Scope and Indel Recovery

To avoid tile-order bias and cross-denominator comparisons, we drew a seeded uniform reservoir sample across all {{audit_pop_nondimer}} qualifying Pipeline B non-dimer read pairs in E19 (`seed={{audit_seed}}`), evaluated *all three alignment methods on the exact same $N = {{c75_n}}$ read pairs*, and archived complete per-read classifications in `data/matched_pair_audit_5000.tsv.gz`:

1. **Matched-pair alignment comparison ($N = {{c75_n}}$ identical non-dimer pairs):**
   - *Paired-end Bowtie2 (on the exact same {{c75_n}} pairs):* Both mates map to the 384-amplicon reference for **{{bm_bt2_pct}}%** of pairs ({{bm_bt2_n}}/{{c75_n}}; 95% Wilson CI: {{bm_bt2_ci}}%), identical to the proper-pair concordant set ({{bm_bt2_conc_n}}/{{c75_n}}, {{bm_bt2_pct}}%). Exactly {{bm_ge60_n}} pairs meet the $\ge 60$ bp length criterion on both mates ({{bm_bt2_ge60_pct}}% mapped under $\ge 60$ bp; {{bm_lt60_n}} pairs have at least one mate $<60$ bp).
   - *Cognate-anchored vs. all-384-target `edlib` ($\ge 75\%$):* Cognate-anchored = **{{c75_cog_on_pct}}%** (95% CI: {{c75_cog_on_ci}}%) vs. dual-strand all-target = **{{c75_all_on_pct}}%** (95% CI: {{c75_all_on_ci}}%); concordance = {{c75_conc_pct}}% ({{c75_conc_ci}}%), with {{c75_cog_off_all_on}} non-cognate reads matching a target (all individually auditable in `data/matched_pair_audit_5000.tsv.gz`). Among cognate-labelled reads (after explicitly collapsing both strands per amplicon), {{c75_cog_cross_best}}/{{c75_cog_tot}} strictly better and {{c75_cog_tied_best}}/{{c75_cog_tot}} tied non-cognate best matches passed the 75% threshold; across all similarity levels, {{audit_strict_below75_n}} cognate-labelled reads exhibited slightly higher non-cognate alignments below both acceptance thresholds ({{audit_strict_below75_alt_range}}% vs. {{audit_strict_below75_cog_range}}%). Against matched paired-end Bowtie2, cognate `edlib` $\ge 75\%$ achieves {{bm_c75_conc}}% concordance ({{bm_c75_conc_ci}}%, $\Delta = {{bm_c75_diff}}$ pp) and all-target `edlib` $\ge 75\%$ achieves {{bm_a75_conc}}% ({{bm_a75_conc_ci}}%, $\Delta = {{bm_a75_diff}}$ pp).
   - *Cognate-anchored vs. all-384-target `edlib` ($\ge 80\%$):* Cognate-anchored = **{{c80_cog_on_pct}}%** (95% CI: {{c80_cog_on_ci}}%) vs. all-target = **{{c80_all_on_pct}}%** (95% CI: {{c80_all_on_ci}}%); concordance = {{c80_conc_pct}}% ({{c80_conc_ci}}%; {{c80_cog_cross_best}} strictly better and {{c80_cog_tied_best}} tied non-cognate best matches passing 80%). Against matched Bowtie2, cognate `edlib` $\ge 80\%$ achieves {{bm_c80_conc}}% concordance ({{bm_c80_conc_ci}}%, $\Delta = {{bm_c80_diff}}$ pp) and all-target `edlib` $\ge 80\%$ achieves {{bm_a80_conc}}% ({{bm_a80_conc_ci}}%, $\Delta = {{bm_a80_diff}}$ pp).

   Single-end `edlib` classified a larger fraction of the matched read pairs as on-target than paired-end Bowtie2. These differences may reflect differences in mate requirements, alignment scoring, target search, and acceptance thresholds.

2. **Gapped vs. ungapped indel recovery ($N = {{ind_n}}$ random cognate reads from {{audit_pop_cognate}}):** Exactly {{ind_rec_n}} reads ({{ind_rec_pct}}%, 95% CI: {{ind_rec_ci}}%) passed gapped `edlib` ($\ge 75\%$) after failing ungapped positional matching ($\ge 75\%$). Full CIGAR parsing showed that **{{ind_gaps_pct}}%** ({{ind_gaps_n}}/{{ind_rec_n}}) contained alignment gaps ({{ind_subonly_n}} substitution-only), with {{ind_le2_n}} ({{ind_le2_pct}}%) having $\le 2$ bp indels and {{ind_gt2_n}} ({{ind_gt2_pct}}%) having $>2$ bp or multi-gap events (median {{ind_med_bp}} bp, IQR {{ind_iqr_lo}}–{{ind_iqr_hi}} bp). This documents alignment gaps under our computational procedure without attributing them to biological insertions or deletions.

# Limitations, Verification, and Availability

Three scope limitations apply. First, 21–30% of raw sequencing pairs are excluded during adapter trimming, quality filtering, and primer matching, and Pipelines A and B retain slightly different read populations (28-bp vs. 17-bp adapter rules and Bowtie2-first vs. primer-first routing); aggregate differences between the two pipelines therefore reflect both alignment logic and retained-read differences. Second, SADDLE's simulated annealing primer-design algorithm is not publicly released and is not replicated here. Third, we did not execute the original scripts inside commercial MATLAB, so minor boundary differences against MATLAB `swalign` cannot be excluded.

To verify end-to-end portability beyond the original Windows workstation, we performed an author-conducted clean-room reproduction in a fresh Kaggle Cloud Linux container (`Linux 6.18.55+`) using an isolated environment created from `environment.yml` (Python 3.12.4, Bowtie2 2.5.5, `edlib` 1.3.9.post1, Matplotlib 3.10.9; distinct from third-party verification by an independent researcher). All 13 external inputs were downloaded and checksum-verified, all 10 unit tests passed, both scientific JSON registries (0 diffs) and all 9 decompressed pair/audit tables matched byte-for-byte, and regenerated PNG figures passed declared cross-platform visual tolerances (`EXIT_CODE=0`; transcripts archived alongside WSL2 Linux logs in `data/verification_logs/`).

In conclusion, our open-source Python + Bowtie2 reimplementation reproduces SADDLE's 96-plex dimer suppression ({{ps1_pa_dimer_pct}}% $\rightarrow$ {{ps2_pa_dimer_pct}}% $\rightarrow$ {{ps3_pa_dimer_pct}}% vs. {{ps1_pub_dimer}}% $\rightarrow$ {{ps2_pub_dimer}}% $\rightarrow$ {{ps3_pub_dimer}}%), 384-plex E19 read partitions ({{e19_pa_ontarget_pct}}% / {{e19_pa_nonspec_pct}}% / {{e19_pa_dimer_pct}}% vs. ~{{e19_pub_ontarget}}% / ~{{e19_pub_nonspec}}% / ~{{e19_pub_dimer_int}}%), and published 96-plex dimer matrices ($r = {{pa_conc_opt_r6_min}}$–${{pa_conc_opt_r6_max}}$, normalized L1 error {{pa_conc_opt_l1_min}}–{{pa_conc_opt_l1_max}}%) without commercial MATLAB. All code (MIT License), pinned environments (`environment.yml`, `requirements-lock-py312.txt`, `requirements.txt`), automated checksum-verified source downloader (`code/download_raw_data.py`), archived directional and unordered pair tables, per-read matched audit evidence, clean-room Kaggle and WSL2 verification logs (`data/verification_logs/`), synchronized LaTeX and Markdown manuscripts, and an automated verification suite (`code/verify_replication.py`, supporting isolated raw-FASTQ recomputation via `--full`) are packaged for release; raw sequencing data remain hosted on Figshare under CC BY 4.0 [@xie2021figshare].

